Title: The Last Train
Author: Sample fixture (draft 2, several defects fixed, one introduced)

FADE IN:

INT. DALLAS UNION STATION - PLATFORM - NIGHT

A cavernous hall, mostly empty. JOHN (40s), rumpled suit, a man who
has clearly slept in worse places, drags a battered suitcase.

ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
Final boarding. Track nine. Final boarding.

JOHN
That's mine. That's always mine.

MEERA (20s), sharp-eyed, in a station vest, blocks his path with a mop.

MEERA
Platform's closed. Floor's wet.

JOHN
I am angry because you are stopping me and that makes me feel frustrated.

MEERA
Then walk around, genius.

The departure board flickers. Every train reads DELAYED except one.

INT. DALLAS UNION STATION - PLATFORM - NIGHT

John hauls the suitcase up the stairs two at a time. He remembers his
father's warning about this exact train and feels a wave of guilt wash
over him.

MEERA
(calling after)
You forgot your ticket!

JOHN
As you know, my brother sold the farm in 2019 after Dad died, which is
why I take this train every single Friday to see the lawyers.

EXT. TRAIN YARD - DAY

Rails stretch to the horizon. John walked along the ballast and looked
for an open door. The suitcase wheels snagged on every tie.

MEERA
Wait up!

EXT. TRAIN YARD - NIGHT

They huddle under a signal gantry. Wind. Somewhere a coupler bangs.

JOHN
You didn't have to follow me.

EXT. TRAIN YARD - NIGHT

The yard again. A freight crawls past, endless.

John counts cars under his breath, lips moving. Forty. Fifty. Sixty.

CLOSE ON the stenciled numbers blurring into one long smear of rust.

RAVI KUMAR
(from a switching shack)
You two lost?

JOHN
Depends who's asking.

INT. SWITCHING SHACK - NIGHT

Cramped. Coffee-stained schedules on every wall.

JOHN
One seat. That's all I need.

EXT. TRAIN YARD - EDGE OF THE FENCE - DAWN

The last train breathes steam. John stops at the open boxcar door,
looks back the way he came.

JOHN
Next Friday, then.

He climbs aboard.

FADE OUT.
